      <category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;Which of the following options is correct?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;func main() {&#xA;    str := &amp;#34;hello&amp;#34;&#xA;    str[0] = &amp;#39;x&amp;#39;&#xA;    fmt.Println(str)&#xA;}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;p&gt;A. hello&#xA;B. xello&#xA;C. compilation error&#xA;&#xA;&lt;details&gt;&#xA;  &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#xA;  &lt;p&gt;Reference code and analysis: C.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Key takeaway: Strings in Go are read-only.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/details&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Go Daily Question 051</title>
      <link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_051/</link>
      <pubDate>Fri, 06 Dec 2024 20:44:30 +0800</pubDate>
      <guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_051/</guid>
      <category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category>
      <category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;What is the output of the following code?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;func main() {  &#xA;    i := -5&#xA;    j := &amp;#43;5&#xA;    fmt.Printf(&amp;#34;%&amp;#43;d %&amp;#43;d&amp;#34;, i, j)&#xA;}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;p&gt;A. -5 +5&#xA;B. +5 +5&#xA;C. 0 0&#xA;&#xA;&lt;details&gt;&#xA;  &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#xA;  &lt;p&gt;A&#xA;&lt;code&gt;%d&lt;/code&gt; indicates outputting a decimal number, and &lt;code&gt;+&lt;/code&gt; indicates outputting the sign of the number. It does not mean negation here.&#xA;&lt;code&gt;%d&lt;/code&gt; output for positive numbers will not include the &lt;code&gt;+&lt;/code&gt; sign by default.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/details&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Go Daily Question 025</title>
      <link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_025/</link>
      <pubDate>Fri, 06 Dec 2024 19:49:56 +0800</pubDate>
      <guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_025/</guid>
      <category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category>
      <category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;Regarding string concatenation, which of the following syntax is correct?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;A. str := &amp;#39;abc&amp;#39; &amp;#43; &amp;#39;123&amp;#39;&#xA;B. str := &amp;#34;abc&amp;#34; &amp;#43; &amp;#34;123&amp;#34;&#xA;C. str := &amp;#39;123&amp;#39; &amp;#43; &amp;#34;abc&amp;#34;&#xA;D. fmt.Sprintf(&amp;#34;abc%d&amp;#34;, 123)&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&lt;details&gt;&#xA;  &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#xA;  &lt;p&gt;Answer: BD&#xA;&lt;code&gt;&#39; &#39;&lt;/code&gt; can only be used for single characters, so A and C will not compile.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;For string concatenation, &lt;code&gt;buffer.WriteString()&lt;/code&gt; is recommended.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/details&gt;</description>
    </item>
